The relationship between counting systems and the development of high number mathematics is foundational, as counting systems evolved from simple methods of tracking quantities to complex numeral systems that enabled abstract mathematical concepts. Early counting systems, such as those using symbols or grouped notations (e.g., base-10), allowed humans to represent larger numbers and establish rules for arithmetic operations [[6](http://alumni.media.mit.edu/~stefanm/society/som_final.html)]. This transition from concrete counting to symbolic representation was critical for advancing mathematics, as it enabled the development of algebra, exponents, and real numbers [[1](https://www.britannica.com/science/numeral)]. 

Cognitive systems like the approximate number system (ANS) and parallel individuation (PI) also play a role, as they provide the innate ability to perceive numerical quantities and distinguish individual items, forming the basis for symbolic number understanding [[3](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27747998/)]. Early number sense, including counting and magnitude comparisons, is a precursor to formal math instruction and underpins higher mathematical thinking [[8](https://mybrainware.com/blog/cognitive-skills-and-math/)]. 

Counting in early education fosters mathematical abilities such as place value, part-whole relationships, and operations, which are essential for advanced mathematics [[9](https://prek-math-te.stanford.edu/counting/mathematics-counting-0)]. Thus, counting systems are not only a precursor to high number mathematics but also a scaffold for developing abstract mathematical reasoning.

